<div class="page-wrap">

  <span class="eyebrow">Reference Guide</span>
  <h1>How to Find a Shoe Style Code on Any Brand</h1>
  <p class="page-sub">When you pick up a shoe at a thrift store, the style code is the fastest way to know what you're holding and what it's worth. It's a unique identifier — part number, part model fingerprint — that tells eBay's search engine exactly which shoe you have. Get it right and you'll find accurate sold listings in seconds. Skip it and you're guessing. This guide covers {brands.length}+ brands and shows you exactly where the style code appears on each one, what it looks like, and what to search. Bookmark it before your next sourcing run.</p>

  <div class="why-section">
    <h2>Why the style code matters</h2>
    <p>Every shoe brand formats its tags differently. Nike puts the code on the tongue label as a six-digit number followed by a dash and three more digits. New Balance puts it under the width designation. Frye uses a five-digit number that's easy to confuse with a longer identifier on the same tag. Knowing which number to search — and which to ignore — is the difference between finding three relevant sold comps and getting zero results.</p>
    <p>The process is simple: pick up the shoe, locate the style code using this guide, type it into eBay's search bar, and filter to Sold listings. What comes back tells you what buyers are actually paying for that exact shoe. That's your pricing baseline before you commit to buying.</p>

    <div class="pro-tip">
      <span class="pro-tip-label">Pro Tip</span>
      <p>Always filter eBay to <strong>Sold listings</strong> — not active listings. Active listings show what sellers are asking. Sold listings show what buyers are actually paying. Those are very different numbers. The style code gets you to the right results. The sold filter shows you the real market.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="brands-header">
    <h2>Style codes by brand</h2>
    <p class="brands-subhead">{brands.length}+ brands — updated from real sourcing experience</p>
  </div>

  <div class="brand-grid">
    {brands.map((brand) => (
      <div class={`brand-card${brand.images.length === 1 ? ' single-image' : ''}`}>
        <div class="brand-card-header">
          <h3 id={brand.slug}>{brand.name} Style Code</h3>
        </div>
        <div class="brand-images">
          {brand.images.map((img) => (
            <img src={`/style-tags/${img}`} alt={`${brand.name} style tag`} loading="lazy" />
          ))}
        </div>
        <div class="brand-card-body">
          <div class="where-to-look">
            <span class="wtl-label">Where to look</span>
            <p set:html={brand.whereToLook} />
          </div>

          {brand.format && (
            <div class="format-row">
              <span class="format-label">Format:</span>
              <span set:html={brand.format} />
            </div>
          )}

          {brand.searchByModelName && (
            <span class="badge-model-name">Search by model name</span>
          )}

          {brand.commonMistake && (
            <div class="common-mistake">
              <span class="mistake-label">⚠️ Common mistake:</span>
              <span set:html={brand.commonMistake} />
            </div>
          )}

          {brand.searchTip && (
            <p class="search-tip" set:html={brand.searchTip} />
          )}

          {brand.note && (
            <p class="card-note" set:html={brand.note} />
          )}
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <div class="bottom-section">
    <h2>How to use this at the thrift store</h2>
    <p>You won't have this page memorized on day one — and you don't need to. Bookmark it on your phone and pull it up while you're standing in the shoe aisle. Pick up the pair, find the brand, locate the style code using the guide, and search eBay sold listings before you commit to buying. After a few sourcing runs you'll have the major brands memorized. The ones you source regularly will become automatic. The ones you see less often are exactly why this page exists.</p>
    <p>A few brands on this list — Fizik, Lake, NoBull, Oakley footwear — don't use traditional style codes. For those, search by model name. It takes a few extra seconds but the results are just as useful.</p>
    <p>One more thing: the style code tells you what shoe you have. The sold listings tell you what it's worth. Neither one alone is enough — you need both before you put that shoe in your cart.</p>
  </div>

